WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.500
AWS S3.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.245
S3 is the universal data substrate.

3
00:00:05.245 --> 00:00:09.367
Datasets, model weights, logs, and backups all end up here.

4
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:12.120
S3 is object storage.

5
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.600
The model is simple.

6
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.100
A bucket, with a globally unique name, holds objects addressed by key.

7
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.815
The namespace is flat.

8
00:00:19.815 --> 00:00:24.833
That path is just one string, and the folders are an illusion drawn by prefix.

9
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:28.600
Storage classes trade cost for access speed.

10
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.833
Standard is fast, and costs the most per gigabyte.

11
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:35.700
Infrequent Access is cheaper to keep, but costlier to read.

12
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.247
Glacier is the cheapest and slowest.

13
00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:41.367
Colder means cheaper per gigabyte, slower to read.

14
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.900
Now the standard way to share a private file.

15
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.433
A browser asks your backend for a file.

16
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:52.067
The backend signs a URL that works for exactly as long as you say.

17
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:58.800
The browser then fetches straight from S3, with no credentials, and your server is out of the download path.

18
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Here is the code, with boto3.

19
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.200
Uploading takes a bucket name and a key, which together are the address.

20
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.782
Then we generate a presigned URL for that object.

21
00:01:09.782 --> 00:01:13.600
It works in any browser, and it expires in sixty seconds.

22
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.130
Two beginner mistakes.

23
00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.967
Treating a prefix like a real folder, and trying to edit bytes inside an object.

24
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:24.120
Keys are flat strings, and objects are immutable.

25
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.867
You overwrite the whole thing.

26
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.300
Count what your stack already keeps in S3.

27
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.700
Training datasets, model weights, raw API payloads, evaluation reports, and trace exports.

28
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.433
A scientist asks for last month's report.

29
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:41.345
Your endpoint doesn't proxy the file.

30
00:01:41.345 --> 00:01:45.933
It returns a sixty second presigned URL, and S3 serves the bytes.

31
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:47.983
Let's recap.

32
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:51.100
S3 is object storage, with buckets and keys.

33
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.667
The namespace is flat, and folders are just prefixes.

34
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:57.233
Storage classes trade cost for speed.

35
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.540
And presigned URLs give temporary access.

36
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.300
Next up: VPC networking.
